  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Karunakaran vs The Ii Class Executive Magistrate-Cum on 24 July, 2023 Author: D.Nagarjun Bench: D.Nagarjun Crl.O.P.(MD) Before The Madurai Bench Of Madras High Court Reserved on 20/9/2023 Delivered on 29/09/2023 Coram: The Honourable Dr.Justice D.Nagarjun Crl.O.P.(MD) No.16155 of 2023 and Crl.M.P(MD)No.12860 of 2023 Karunakaran ... Petitione Vs. 1.The Ii Class Executive Magistrate-cum- The Tahsildar, Paramakudi, Ramanathapuram District. 2.The State rep. by The Inspector of Police (Law & Order), G4, Nainarkoil Police Station, Ramanathapuram District. ...Respondent Prayer :",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "The Honble Supreme Court of India in the case of Gopalanachari v. State of Kerala, reported in 1980 (Supp) SCC 649. \"The constitutional survival of Section 110 certainly depends on its obedience to Article 21, as this Court has expounded. Words of wide import, vague amplitude and far too generalised to be safe in the hands of the Police cannot be constitutionalised in the context of Article 21 unless read down to be as a fair and reasonable legislation with reverence for human rights. A glance at Section 110 shows that only a narrow signification can be attached to the words in clauses (a) to (g), \"by habit a robber....\", \"by habit a receiver of stolen property....\", \"habitually protects or harbours thieve....\", \"habitually commits or attempts to commit or abets the commission of .... \", \"is so desperate and dangerous as to render his being at large without security hazardous to the community\". The poor are picked up or brought up, habitual witnesses swear away their freedom and courts ritualistically commit them to prison and Article 21 is for them a freedom under total eclipse in practice. After all, the judicial process must not fail functionally as the protector of personal liberty.\"",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "The Honble Supreme Court of India in the case of Gopalanachari v. State of Kerala, reported in 1980 (Supp) SCC 649. \"The constitutional survival of Section 110 certainly depends on its obedience to Article 21, as this Court has expounded. Words of wide import, vague amplitude and far too generalised to be safe in the hands of the Police cannot be constitutionalised in the context of Article 21 unless read down to be as a fair and reasonable legislation with reverence for human rights. A glance at Section 110 shows that only a narrow signification can be attached to the words in clauses (a) to (g), \"by habit a robber....\", \"by habit a receiver of stolen property....\", \"habitually protects or harbours thieve....\", \"habitually commits or attempts to commit or abets the commission of .... \", \"is so desperate and dangerous as to render his being at large without security hazardous to the community\". The poor are picked up or brought up, habitual witnesses swear away their freedom and courts ritualistically commit them to prison and Article 21 is for them a freedom under total eclipse in practice. After all, the judicial process must not fail functionally as the protector of personal liberty.\""
  },
